    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The Last Pictures Taken Inside the World Trade Center"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In the summer of 2001, a night-shift electrician wandered the empty floors of the World Trade Center with a digital camera, photographing offices, kitchens and stairwells no one else thought to record. Twenty-five years later, for the anniversary of September 11, Blind publishes this set of historical images."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "By Jonas Cuénin. Photos by Konstantin Petrov."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/petrov-portrait.webp",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Konstantin Petrov arrived in New York from Estonia in 1998, working unlicensed electrical jobs before he landed steadier work. In June 2001, he took a night-shift position at Windows on the World, the restaurant occupying the 106th and 107th floors of the North Tower. By day he was the superintendent of a Manhattan apartment building; by night, alone in the towers after the offices emptied out, he carried his camera."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/dscn8252-vi.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/dscn8485-vi.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/hr3975qbgaaemng.webp",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Over that summer he shot hundreds of digital photographs: banquettes and table settings laid for the next day’s service, elevator fixtures, sconces, stairwells, kitchen equipment. From more than 1,300 feet up, his eye also caught dawn breaking over a dormant Manhattan, the city visible through glass no one else was looking through at that hour."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/dscn7898-vi.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/dscn8270-vi.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/dscn8276-vi.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Petrov shared the images on Fotki, an early Estonian photo-sharing site started by his friend Dmitri Don. “World Trade Center is, like, so cool,” Don later recalled the two of them saying when Petrov got the job, a line he still gave years afterward, once the towers were gone and the remark had taken on a different weight."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/dscn8273-vi.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/dscn8271-vi.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/dscn8166-vi.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "On the morning of September 11, Petrov finished his shift and drove out of the building’s basement parking garage. He was already gone when the first plane struck, and didn’t grasp what had happened until he reached home and turned on the news."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/dscn8185-1-vi.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/dscn8191-vi.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "url": "https://www.blind-magazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/dscn8163-vi.jpg",
      "alt": "",
      "caption": "",
      "credit": "Blind Magazine"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The photographs sat unnoticed until 2014, when documentary filmmaker Erik Nelson, researching a National Geographic film called 9/10: The Final Hours , found Petrov’s Fotki archive and used 44 of the images. Petrov never saw that recognition: he died in a motorcycle accident on Manhattan’s West Side Highway in 2002, less than a year after the attacks."
    },
